+---
+id: dl-005-createpatch-semantics-minimal-diff-lcs-arrays-pure-strict-json-input
+type: decision-log
+title: "createPatch semantics: minimal diff, LCS arrays, pure, strict JSON input"
+status: pending
+---
+
+<!-- decision-log body. `wingfoil memory add` copies this scaffold verbatim; `memory submit` replaces
+     these placeholder comments with real content and fills the required frontmatter fields. -->
+
+## Context
+
+Clients want to send only what changed between two document versions. RFC 6902 does not say how to
+compute a patch, so the size and shape of the result is ours to define.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Objects are compared member by member and recursed into; only scalars and values of different
+  types are replaced as a whole. Equal values yield `[]`.
+- Arrays are diffed by longest common subsequence, so an insertion or removal does not rewrite the
+  elements after it; a removed/added pair at the same position is diffed recursively.
+- Only `add`, `remove` and `replace` are emitted: no `move`/`copy` detection, which costs more
+  to compute and is not needed for a correct, small patch.
+- Pure and deterministic: inputs are not mutated, values in the patch are cloned, operation order
+  follows key order (removals first, then additions/changes in the key order of `to`).
+- Inputs must be JSON values (same check as `applyPatch`), otherwise it throws. Member names are
+  escaped as RFC 6901 requires, and own members only are considered.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Array diffing is O(n*m) in time and memory for the compared arrays. Patches are not guaranteed to be
+the absolute smallest possible, since moves are expressed as remove + add.
